board_attiny3217_leds — modern-AVR OpenGL LED window
====================================================

The *visual* tinyAVR-1 board: it opens a real window (OpenGL/GLUT) showing the
eight PORTA pins of an ATtiny3217 as eight LEDs, animated live by the firmware —
a single lit LED bounces back and forth ("Knight Rider"). USART0 narration is
echoed to the terminal.

This is the tinyAVR-1 sibling of board_atmega4809_leds, and the GUI counterpart
to the headless examples:
  * board_attiny3217 / board_atmega4809 — console + VCD demos
  * board_modern_avr                    — all-cores coverage runner
Same modern engine and peripheral models, but with something to watch.

Build & run (from this folder)
------------------------------

Open a console, change into this folder, build, then run:

    cd examples/board_attiny3217_leds   # the demo loads its .axf from here
    make                                # builds the host program + firmware (.axf)
    ./obj-*/ledramp.elf                 # run the demo (opens the window)

The runnable program is the host binary built under obj-<arch>/ (for example
obj-x86_64-pc-linux-gnu/ledramp.elf); the ./obj-*/ glob just picks it regardless
of architecture. Run it from this folder so it finds attiny3217_ledramp.axf.

A window titled "simavr - attiny3217 PORTA LEDs" appears with 8 LEDs; the lit
one sweeps left/right. The terminal shows the firmware's USART0 output ("LED N").
Press 'q' or Esc in the window to quit. (A graphical display is required, since
this board opens an OpenGL window.)

How it works
------------

  * The firmware (attiny3217_ledramp.c) drives all of PORTA as outputs and walks
    a single set bit across it. USART0 TXD uses its default route (PB2), which is
    already off the PORTA LED pins on the tinyAVR-1 — so, unlike the megaAVR-0
    sibling, no PORTMUX change is needed.

  * The host (ledramp.c) hooks the eight PORTA pin IRQs to a bitmap and draws
    them as LEDs, hooks USART0 to stdout, and runs the AVR in a background
    thread that is *throttled to real time* (compares simulated cycles to the
    wall clock and sleeps the difference) so the 120 ms steps are watchable
    instead of flashing past at simulation speed.

The PORT and USART hooks are ordinary avr_irq_register_notify() calls on the
classic IOPORT / UART IRQ meshes — the modern PORT and USART models reuse those
abstractions, so the GUI plumbing is identical to simavr's classic GL boards
(board_ledramp, board_hd44780).

Requirements
------------

Needs OpenGL + GLUT (freeglut) dev libraries, like the other visual boards; the
Makefile pulls them in via ../Makefile.opengl. Building the firmware needs a
modern avr-gcc (12+) with the tinyAVR-1 headers; if avr-gcc cannot target
attiny3217 the firmware step is skipped (so a plain `make` across examples/ still
succeeds) — install a modern avr-gcc and re-run `make` to build and run the demo.
